 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** context-local proxies. Each name is a `werkzeug.local.LocalProxy` over a single `ContextVar`. On every access the proxy reads the var's current value and forwards to an attribute of it.\n\n**`src/tessel/globals.py`** (lines 40–62):\n- `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext]` holds the current context.\n- `active_app` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.app` (the `Tessel` instance).\n- `g` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.g` (an `_AppCtxGlobals`).\n- `request` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.request`.\n- `app_ctx` is the proxy for the context itself. `session` works the same way.\n\n**`src/tessel/ctx.py`:** `AppContext.push()` (line ~416) calls `_app_var.set(self)` and stores the token in `_cv_token`. `pop()` (line ~446) calls `_app_var.reset(token)`. Because it is a `ContextVar`, each thread or async task sees its own context, and nested pushes restore the previous one on pop.\n\nOutside a context the var is unset, so the proxies raise the `unbound_message` errors (\"Working outside of application context\" or \"…request context\").\n\n`request_ctx` is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`, since the request and app contexts have merged into one.",
